  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nBroad surface high pressure across the region this morning is \nexpected to shift southeastward today with southwest to northeast \nflow developing in the near surface layer.  This will bring the \nstratus across eastern KS back into the region today.  The stubborn \ncloud cover and weak flow will keep temperatures cool once again \ntoday, and have lowered forecast highs into the low and mid 50s.  \n\nLow clouds are are expected to clear the region tonight; however, \nthe southwest flow will remain ahead of a weak boundary approaching \nfrom the north. Models are hinting at the potential for patchy fog \ndeveloping across northern Missouri, but increasing high clouds \nbuilding south may limit the radiative potential, so for now, have \nleft the mention out of the forecast.  With dry airmass, expect \ntemperatures to fall back into the mid 30s across central into \neastern Missouri leading to another frosty night. \n\nExpect warmer conditions on Sunday as 925 hpa temperatures warm 8-10 \nC over Saturday's values. With just high clouds across the\nregion, should be a fairly pleasant fall day. \n\nWeak boundaries associated with systems moving east across Canada \nare expected to approach every few days; however, broad high \npressure across much of the lower 48 are expected to limit their \nimpact. A series of phased waves lead to the development of a \ndeepening trough mid-week across the Great Lakes into the Tennessee \nValley. While the precipitation with this system looks to remain \neast of the area, cooler temperatures are expected Thursday into \nnext weekend are expected with north-northwesterly flow. \nTemperatures moderate towards seasonal norms next weekend as trough \nbuilds toward the east coast, but with the developing blocking \npattern, system may not be in a hurry to move east.",
